{% extends "base.html.j2" %}

{% block title %}The field, year by year &mdash; {{ config.site.title }}{% endblock %}

{% block meta %}
{% set social_title = 'The field, year by year' %}
{% set social_url = config.site.base_url ~ '/trends/' %}
{% set social_description %}How submissions, acceptance rates, open access,
  anonymous reviewing and artifact evaluation have moved across
  {{ trends.editions }} recorded editions, {{ trends.first }} to
  {{ trends.last }}.{% endset %}
<meta name="description" content="{{ social_description | oneline }}">
<link rel="canonical" href="{{ social_url }}">
{% include "_social.html.j2" %}
{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<article>

<header class="page-head">
  <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">All conferences</a> / Trends</p>
  <h1 class="page-title">The field, year by year</h1>
  <p class="page-sub">What {{ trends.editions }} recorded editions, {{ trends.first }}
    to {{ trends.last }}, can and cannot say about how things have changed.</p>
</header>

<section class="panel">
  {# The caveat comes before the charts, not after them in small print. A
     reader who takes one line away should take this one. #}
  <h2 class="panel-title">What this is, and what it is not</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>Nothing here is a count.</b> Coverage varies enormously by year, so
      a count of editions doing artifact evaluation would climb through the
      2010s whatever the field did, purely because recent years are better
      entered.</p>
    <p><b>Nor is it a rate among the editions that answer.</b> That was the
      obvious fix and it is worse, because whether a field is recorded is not
      independent of its answer. Nobody writes down <code>artifact =
      false</code>; the key appears when there <i>was</i> an artifact track and
      is simply absent otherwise. A rate over the editions that answered
      therefore came out near 100%, which said a great deal about how this
      corpus is typed and nothing about the field.</p>
    <p>So each bar below is <b>every edition of that year</b>, split three
      ways: recorded as doing it, recorded as not, and nobody has checked. The
      solid part is a <b>floor</b> &mdash; at least this many &mdash; and the
      dotted part is what decides how much that floor is worth. A practice at
      20% solid with half the column dotted might really be at 60%; the chart
      says so by showing both rather than picking one.</p>
    <p>Two charts work differently and say so under themselves: the medians
      can only be taken over editions that publish a figure, and the gender
      line is a rate per person over the editions that record a committee
      &mdash; whether somebody looked up a committee does not depend on who
      was on it.</p>
    <p>None of this makes the corpus a sample of the field. It is what one
      person had time to enter: it leans towards formal methods, and within
      that towards conferences that publish their numbers at all. Where a year
      holds fewer than {{ trends.floor }} editions, nothing is drawn.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel">
  <h2 class="panel-title">Coverage</h2>
  <p class="chart-caption">How much of each year is here. This is a fact about
    the records, not about the field &mdash; it is the denominator for
    everything that follows, and the reason none of it is a count.</p>
  <div class="trendstrip">
    {% set peak = trends.rows | map(attribute='editions') | max %}
    <ol class="trendbars">
      {% for r in trends.rows %}
      <li title="{{ r.year }}: {{ r.editions }} edition{{ 's' if r.editions != 1 }} of {{ r.series }} conference{{ 's' if r.series != 1 }}">
        <i style="--h: {{ (100 * r.editions / peak) | round | int }}%"></i></li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ol>
    {% include "_trendaxis.html.j2" %}
  </div>
</section>

{% for m in trends.metrics %}
{% set field = {'pct': 'floor', 'rate': 'pct'}.get(m.kind, 'value') %}
<section class="panel">
  <h2 class="panel-title">{{ m.label }}</h2>
  <div class="trendstrip">
    <ol class="trendbars{{ ' is-stacked' if m.kind == 'pct' }}">
      {% for r in trends.rows %}
      {% set cell = r[m.key] %}
      {% set value = cell[field] %}
      {% if value is none %}
      <li class="is-thin" title="{{ r.year }}: too few editions recorded to draw"></li>
      {% elif m.kind == 'pct' %}
      {# Three parts of every edition that year, not a rate among the ones that
         answered. Whether a field is recorded is not independent of its answer
         — nobody writes `artifact = false` — so the solid part is a floor, and
         the dotted part is what decides how much that floor is worth. #}
      <li title="{{ r.year }}: {{ cell.yes }} of {{ cell.total }} editions recorded as doing this
        {%- if cell.no %}, {{ cell.no }} recorded as not{% endif %}
        {%- if cell.unknown %}, {{ cell.unknown }} say nothing{% endif %} — at least {{ value }}%">
        <i class="seg-yes" style="--h: {{ (100 * cell.yes / cell.total) | round(1) }}%"></i>
        <i class="seg-no" style="--h: {{ (100 * cell.no / cell.total) | round(1) }}%"></i>
        <i class="seg-unknown" style="--h: {{ (100 * cell.unknown / cell.total) | round(1) }}%"></i>
      </li>
      {% else %}
      {% set height = (100 * value / (100 if m.kind == 'rate' else m.peak)) | round | int %}
      <li title="{{ r.year }}: {{ value }}{{ m.unit }} of {{ cell.n }} edition{{ 's' if cell.n != 1 }}{% if m.key == 'women' %}, {{ cell.people }} people{% endif %}">
        <i style="--h: {{ [height, 2] | max }}%"></i></li>
      {% endif %}
      {% endfor %}
    </ol>
    {% include "_trendaxis.html.j2" %}
  </div>
  {% if m.kind == 'pct' %}
  <p class="trendkey" aria-hidden="true">
    <span><i class="seg-yes"></i> recorded as doing it</span>
    <span><i class="seg-no"></i> recorded as not</span>
    <span><i class="seg-unknown"></i> nobody has checked</span>
  </p>
  {% endif %}
  {% set drawn = trends.rows | selectattr(m.key ~ '.' ~ field) | list %}
  {% if drawn %}
  <p class="trendends">
    <span>{{ drawn[0].year }}: <b>{% if m.kind == 'pct' %}&ge;&#8201;{% endif %}{{ drawn[0][m.key][field] }}{{ m.unit }}</b></span>
    <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
    <span>{{ drawn[-1].year }}: <b>{% if m.kind == 'pct' %}&ge;&#8201;{% endif %}{{ drawn[-1][m.key][field] }}{{ m.unit }}</b></span>
    {% if m.kind != 'value' %}<span class="data">peak {{ m.peak }}%</span>{% endif %}
  </p>
  {% endif %}
  <p class="chart-caption">
    {%- if m.note %}{{ m.note }} {% endif -%}
    {% if m.kind == 'pct' %}Each column is every edition of that year. The solid
    part is a <b>floor</b> &mdash; at least this many did it &mdash; because an
    edition that says nothing may have done it too and simply never been
    checked.{% endif %}
    {%- if m.thin %} {{ m.thin }} year{{ 's' if m.thin != 1 }} left blank for
    want of editions.{% endif %}</p>
</section>
{% endfor %}

<section class="panel">
  <h2 class="panel-title">The numbers</h2>
  <p class="chart-caption">Every figure above. The four right-hand columns are
    floors over <i>all</i> editions of that year, with the number that record
    the field at all beside them: <code>12/48</code> means twelve editions
    answered out of forty-eight, so the percentage can only go up as the rest
    are checked. Women and the two medians are taken over the editions that
    record them, which is stated with each chart.</p>
  <table class="editions sortable" id="trendtable">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th data-sort="number" aria-sort="ascending">Year</th>
        <th class="num" data-sort="number">Editions</th>
        <th class="num" data-sort="number">Confs</th>
        <th class="num" data-sort="number">Subs <small>median</small></th>
        <th class="num" data-sort="number">Rate</th>
        <th class="num" data-sort="number">Women</th>
        <th class="num" data-sort="number">Free</th>
        <th class="num" data-sort="number">Open</th>
        <th class="num" data-sort="number">Anon.</th>
        <th class="num" data-sort="number">Artifact</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for r in trends.rows %}
      <tr>
        <td><b>{{ r.year }}</b></td>
        <td class="num">{{ r.editions }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ r.series }}</td>
        <td class="num">{% if r.submitted.value is not none %}{{ r.submitted.value }}
          <small>{{ r.submitted.n }}</small>{% else %}&ndash;{% endif %}</td>
        <td class="num">{% if r.rate.value is not none %}{{ r.rate.value }}%
          <small>{{ r.rate.n }}</small>{% else %}&ndash;{% endif %}</td>
        <td class="num">{% if r.women.pct is not none %}{{ r.women.pct }}%
          <small>{{ r.women.n }}</small>{% else %}&ndash;{% endif %}</td>
        {# A floor over every edition, with how many of them said anything. #}
        {% for key in ('free', 'open', 'double', 'artifact') %}
        <td class="num">{% if r[key].floor is not none %}&ge;&#8201;{{ r[key].floor }}%
          <small>{{ r[key].n }}/{{ r[key].total }}</small>{% else %}&ndash;{% endif %}</td>
        {% endfor %}
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<section class="panel">
  <h2 class="panel-title">Taking this further</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The underlying records are <a href="/data/">available as JSON and
      CSV</a>, so none of this has to be taken on trust: the same counts can be
      recomputed, and recomputed differently. If a figure here looks wrong, it
      probably is &mdash; <a href="/submit/">corrections</a> are the main way
      this stays accurate.</p>
  </div>
</section>

</article>
{% endblock %}

{% block scripts %}
{# The tables carry `sortable`, which does nothing without this. #}
<script src="/assets/places.js?v={{ asset_version }}" defer></script>
{% endblock %}
